          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<h2>The simplest definition</h2><p>Probiotics are <strong>live microorganisms</strong> — primarily bacteria and yeasts — that, when consumed in adequate amounts, deliver measurable health benefits. They're often called \"good bacteria\" or \"friendly bacteria\" because they support the balance and function of the trillions of microbes that already live in your gut, mouth, skin, and elsewhere on your body.</p><p>The most common probiotic strains belong to two genera: <em>Lactobacillus</em> and <em>Bifidobacterium</em>. These are the workhorses behind digestion, immunity, mood regulation, and inflammation control.</p>"
          }
        },
