        "t2": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<h2>What do probiotics actually do?</h2><p>Your body hosts roughly 38 trillion microbial cells — about the same as your own human cells. Most of them live in your gut. Together they form your microbiome.</p><p>A balanced microbiome:</p><ul><li><strong>Digests food efficiently</strong> — extracts nutrients, breaks down fibres your enzymes can't.</li><li><strong>Trains your immune system</strong> — 70% of immunity originates in the gut.</li><li><strong>Produces vitamins</strong> — including B12, K2, and folate.</li><li><strong>Regulates mood</strong> — 90% of your serotonin is made in the gut, not the brain.</li><li><strong>Keeps pathogens at bay</strong> — good bacteria outcompete the bad ones for space and resources.</li></ul><p>When your microbiome is depleted — by antibiotics, stress, processed food, sugar, or illness — every one of these functions suffers.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t3": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<h2>Why supplement?</h2><p>In a perfect world, your diet would maintain your microbiome through fermented foods, fibre, and diverse plant matter. In reality, modern diets are stripped of fibre, heavy in sugar, and rarely include traditional ferments like kimchi, kefir, sauerkraut, or live yoghurt.</p><p>That's where probiotic supplements come in — concentrated, clinically-researched strains delivered in doses you'd struggle to get from food alone. But only if they actually reach your gut alive.</p>"
          }
        },
